# Waveshare PICO-Cam-A

Select board `pico_cam_a` on the `pico-arm` SDK lane. The board derives from
`pico`, but the SDK bridge selects `waveshare_pico_cam_a` for the correct 16
MB flash capacity. The board binds mm.display to its ST7789 LCD and mm.camera
to its HM01B0 camera; neither provider links into an application unless it
reaches that interface.

Build and verify a camera-to-LCD image:

```sh
scripts/build-pico.sh --board pico_cam_a --app camera-demo
scripts/build-pico.sh --board pico_cam_a --app camera-demo --flash
scripts/build-pico.sh --board pico_cam_a --app gfx-demo
```

The scripts require the usual Pico tools and picotool package. `--dry-run`
checks lane selection and provider closure without those tools or hardware.
Put the board in BOOTSEL before using `--flash`.

`camera-demo` automatically starts live 324 by 324 grayscale capture in
CROP after reset (WAIT during exposure settling, then LIVE). Press K3
(GP18) to stop or restart capture on the 240 by 135 LCD. Stopping puts
the sensor in standby and keeps the last image on screen. K4 (GP19) cycles
CROP (the default), NEAREST, AREA, and AREA+TIME. CROP matches Waveshare's
unscaled x=0..239 and reversed y=134..0 display window. K4 also works while
paused, reprocessing the retained frame.

NEAREST and AREA resize the full square frame with the same vertical
orientation. AREA averages fractional source-pixel coverage when resizing.
AREA+TIME also blends each new frame equally with the previous output,
reducing random noise at the cost of trails on moving objects. History resets
on restart and mode changes. The first eight frames after each start are
discarded to allow automatic exposure to settle. These are application
processing modes; sensor gain and exposure settings remain automatic.

The top 17 rows display STOP, WAIT (startup), LIVE, or ERR3/ERR4/ERR8
(initialization/capture/standby failure), plus the mode and button actions. K3
retries a stopped camera after an error. Buttons have release debouncing and
falling-edge latches to retain taps during capture. Actions take effect
between frames; a capture timeout is bounded to 500 ms.

The camera uses I2C0 GP4/GP5 (address 0x24), data GP6, PCLK GP14, LVLD GP15,
and FVLD GP16. MCLK comes from the board's 36 MHz oscillator. The adapter uses
the gated pixel clock and MSB-first serial output, claims one PIO state
machine plus six instructions and one DMA channel, and bounds I2C transfers
and frame capture. Frame synchronization and transfer share a capture
deadline. A timeout stops DMA; the next call starts from a fresh FVLD edge.
`sleep()` releases resources even if the sensor cannot acknowledge standby.
Call this API from one core. Camera-linked applications set the core regulator
to 1.15 V and clk_sys to 250 MHz at startup, before peripheral configuration,
matching the working Waveshare demo's clock. This exceeds the datasheet 200
MHz operating point. The clock stays at 250 MHz when capture stops. This gives
the ArduCAM-style four-instruction sampling loop 6.94 PIO cycles per 36 MHz
serial bit. Every sensor initialization write gets the reference 10 ms
settling delay. LCD pixels and status are sent in one full-frame write. The
previous 125 MHz sequence could sample late and its 0x3060=0x20 setting had
incorrect serial bit order; the original 0x30 setting is restored. See
boards/pico_cam_a/mm.mdy for the timing comparison. Basic preview has been confirmed on the board.
Frame rate and image quality are not formally qualified.

The LCD is SPI1 on GP10/GP11, CS GP13, DC GP9, reset GP7, with a permanently
powered backlight. Do not use the vendor demo's LCD_BL=14, reset=2 or XCLK=3:
the schematic connects GP14 to camera PCLK and exposes GP2/GP3 as I2C1. UART0
is GP0/GP1. K3 and K4 are active-low buttons on GP18 and GP19; the board has
no user LED.

Original camera support: [ArduCAM/RPI-Pico-Cam](https://github.com/ArduCAM/RPI-Pico-Cam), with provenance and local
adaptations recorded in boards/pico_cam_a/mm.mdy.

Board sources: [Waveshare documentation](https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/PICO-Cam-A), [schematic](https://files.waveshare.com/wiki/PICO-Cam-A/PICO-Cam-A.pdf), [reference code](https://files.waveshare.com/wiki/PICO-Cam-A/PICO-Cam-A.zip).

Host regression checks:

```sh
sh test.sh --host tests/mm/camera/preview/
sh tests/scripts/run.sh
```

Firmware builds and UF2 checks do not replace a test on an actual board.
